WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:05.320
So now what is happening here?

00:05.320 --> 00:09.200
I would like to show the orders, but only from customers from USA.

00:09.920 --> 00:14.160
So if you check this query over here, we are joining the tables order and customers.

00:14.160 --> 00:20.280
But mainly we are showing only the orders information and that means we are using the customers only

00:20.280 --> 00:22.040
to filter the table orders.

00:22.040 --> 00:25.320
And there are like multiple ways on how to do this task.

00:25.320 --> 00:29.800
So it's not only the joins, you can go and use the exists as a subquery.

00:30.040 --> 00:34.200
And as well you can go and use the in operator in the subquery.

00:34.240 --> 00:39.120
And now comes the old but gold question which one is better.

00:39.120 --> 00:42.080
Should we join or use exist or in.

00:42.080 --> 00:42.840
And oh my god.

00:42.840 --> 00:47.960
If you go to the forums you will see people fighting about which one is the best.

00:49.320 --> 00:51.840
Ah come on, do that again.

00:52.320 --> 00:53.720
Do that again I dare ya.

00:54.000 --> 00:56.240
Ah okay.

00:57.840 --> 01:02.800
Oh oh you can't see, you can't see 1.2 points.

01:02.840 --> 01:04.360
Now about the best practices.

01:04.360 --> 01:08.480
Everyone agrees that don't go and use the in operator.

01:08.480 --> 01:11.080
So this is the bad practice.

01:11.080 --> 01:15.400
So bad practice, avoid it, don't use it.

01:15.400 --> 01:19.680
And of course I'm always speaking about big tables, okay, not small tables.

01:19.680 --> 01:25.840
So we don't go and use this in order to filter one table based on the result of another table.

01:25.840 --> 01:28.400
So don't use an operator in this scenario.

01:28.440 --> 01:32.280
Now here comes the conflicts we have join and exist.

01:32.280 --> 01:34.360
Well about the performance of those two.

01:34.600 --> 01:39.800
They are very similar for medium tables like I'm speaking about hundreds or thousands and so on.

01:39.800 --> 01:41.280
But still you have to test it.

01:41.280 --> 01:43.640
You have to go and compare the execution plans.

01:43.640 --> 01:50.800
And if you are getting like identical results and both of them are having the same speed, then I prefer

01:50.840 --> 01:52.040
to go with the join.

01:52.080 --> 01:56.680
And that's because, to be honest, it is easier to write than writing that exists.

01:56.680 --> 02:06.760
So I'm gonna say from my point of view, this is best practice if the performance equal Two exists.

02:07.280 --> 02:12.480
But now what happens for me is that sometimes I get better performance using exists.

02:12.480 --> 02:19.160
So I'm gonna say from my point of view, the best practice here and now you might ask why we are getting

02:19.160 --> 02:21.800
with the exist better performance than the inner join.

02:21.800 --> 02:27.080
And that's because SQL has only to check the existence of data from the subquery.

02:27.080 --> 02:32.920
But in the other hand, with the inner join, SQL has to go and start doing matching between two tables.

02:32.920 --> 02:36.520
So it's going to go and evaluate all matching records and so on.

02:36.720 --> 02:39.920
It is not evaluating whether it exists or not.

02:39.960 --> 02:46.000
And as well sometimes SQL has to deal with more rows because you might introduce duplicates as you are

02:46.040 --> 02:46.960
joining tables.

02:46.960 --> 02:49.400
And this will not happen using exists.

02:49.400 --> 02:55.440
So for some scenarios if you are using exist you might get better performance than using join, but

02:55.440 --> 02:58.360
everyone agrees to not use the in operator.

02:58.400 --> 03:02.640
Okay, the next tip is to avoid redundant logic in your query.

03:02.640 --> 03:08.110
This happens a lot if you have a lot of subqueries, and if you analyze it, you might find sometimes

03:08.110 --> 03:09.630
there are like redundancy.

03:09.630 --> 03:15.190
So for example this query I would like to have like a tag for each employee, whether the salary is

03:15.190 --> 03:17.230
above the average or below the average.

03:17.350 --> 03:18.990
So now we might do it like this.

03:19.030 --> 03:24.030
We say okay, let's get the data for employees where the salary is higher than the average, and you

03:24.030 --> 03:26.550
go and calculate the average in a sub query.

03:26.550 --> 03:29.430
So if it's higher then you write here above average.

03:29.430 --> 03:31.710
And now we say okay let's go for the below average.

03:31.710 --> 03:37.230
So we do a union all and that condition is going to be salary is less than the average.

03:37.270 --> 03:40.070
And now by checking this you see that there is a problem.

03:40.070 --> 03:43.510
First of all we are querying the employees like four times.

03:43.510 --> 03:46.070
We have 1234.

03:46.230 --> 03:49.630
So we are scanning the table employees four times.

03:49.670 --> 03:51.510
And as well we have the same logic over here.

03:51.510 --> 03:55.070
So we are calculating the average of salary at twice.

03:55.070 --> 03:57.830
So this is of course I can say a bad practice.

03:57.830 --> 04:00.870
And there is like many ways on how to do it better than that.

04:00.870 --> 04:05.790
For example, you can go and put this subquery in a CTE and then use it multiple times.

04:05.790 --> 04:08.590
But there is like better solution using the window function.

04:08.590 --> 04:10.310
So if you check this it is very simple.

04:10.310 --> 04:11.510
It's me executed.

04:11.550 --> 04:16.470
We are reading the table employees only once and then we are using the case statements.

04:16.470 --> 04:19.510
If the salary is higher than the window function.

04:19.510 --> 04:23.070
So we are calculating the average on top of the whole table employees.

04:23.470 --> 04:27.510
If it's higher, then right above average if it's lower than below average.

04:27.510 --> 04:30.630
So as you can see it is easier to read and it is smaller.

04:30.630 --> 04:35.870
And the performance here is way better than reading four times the employees and repeating the same

04:35.870 --> 04:36.430
logic.

04:36.430 --> 04:41.310
So here you have always to look to your queries, and if you see that you are repeating the same things

04:41.310 --> 04:44.270
over and over, then you are writing a bad query.

04:44.310 --> 04:50.390
Think about alternatives like CTE window functions, and I'm sure you will find a better way than reading

04:50.390 --> 04:53.990
the table several times or repeating the same logic several times.

04:53.990 --> 04:58.870
So as you can see, optimizing the queries is not always about using indexes and partitions.

04:58.870 --> 05:01.430
It's all about using best practices.

05:01.470 --> 05:06.590
All right guys, so with that, we have covered a lot of best practices on how to optimize the performance

05:06.590 --> 05:07.430
of your query.

05:07.430 --> 05:09.870
And as you can see it's not always creating indexes.

05:09.870 --> 05:10.310
Right.

05:10.310 --> 05:11.190
In many scenarios.

05:11.190 --> 05:13.270
It's about how you write the query.
